WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.600
Bada bing! Fourteen years after the 86th and&nbsp;
final episode of the landmark HBO mob series "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.520
Sopranos," the franchise is back with the prequel&nbsp;
"The Many Saints of Newark". Here are a few small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.520
details in the film that viewers may have missed.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.320
Fans should pay close attention to the voice of&nbsp;
the person who provides the opening narration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.000
"The Many Saints of Newark." If that voice sounds&nbsp;
familiar, there's a very good reason: It's Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.440
Imperioli, the actor who played Christopher&nbsp;
Moltisanti, and the only original member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.920
"Sopranos" cast to be involved in the prequel.
The character is speaking from beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.320
grave as the opening shot in the film&nbsp;
sweeps over tombstones in a graveyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.880
until it lands on Christopher's headstone. The&nbsp;
narrator goes on to recount how his mentor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.520
Tony Soprano, would go on to murder him.
For those who need a brutal reminder of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.080
things went down, you can revisit the tragic scene&nbsp;
when Tony chokes Christopher to death instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.000
of calling 911 following their car accident in&nbsp;
"Kennedy and Heidi," which is the 18th episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.680
of "The Sopranos" Season 6.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.240
In one ironic scene, Dickie Moltisanti&nbsp;
introduces baby Christopher during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.040
"family" dinner attended by Tony. Given the&nbsp;
close relationship Dickie has with Tony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.080
he tries to hand off his new baby, Christopher,&nbsp;
to his young protégé, and the infant responds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.360
with a crying fit in front of his future mentor.
In a subtle bit of foreshadowing of Christopher's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.280
untimely death at Tony's hands in "The&nbsp;
Sopranos," the young Tony responds to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.760
crying infant by saying, "It's like I scare&nbsp;
him or something." After that, an elderly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.360
dinner guest responds with this bit of wisdom:
"Some babies, when they come into the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.880
know all kinds of things from the other side."
It's a sobering observation to contemplate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.945
next time you encounter a colicky baby.

00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:53.840
"The Sopranos" was never afraid of being meta&nbsp;
when it came to references to classic mob movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.400
including several tips of the cap to Francis&nbsp;
Ford Coppola's "Godfather" trilogy and Martin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.320
Scorsese's "Goodfellas." Scorsese seemingly&nbsp;
appears in a cameo in "46 Long," the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.760
episode of Season 1, although the famed director&nbsp;
is actually being played by actor Anthony Caso.&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.920
The biggest nod to "Goodfellas" throughout&nbsp;
the series, though, is the casting of Lorraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.920
Bracco as Tony's therapist, Dr. Jennifer Melfi.&nbsp;
In "Goodfellas," Bracco stars as Karen Hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.080
the long-suffering wife of real-life mobster Henry&nbsp;
Hill, played by Ray Liotta. Among the impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:31.600
ensemble cast of "The Many Saints of Newark"&nbsp;
is Liotta, who stars as Dickie Moltisanti's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.960
fiery father "Hollywood Dick" Moltisanti. While&nbsp;
Liotta's appearance is far from a bona fide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.560
"Goodfellas" reunion with Bracco, it rather serves&nbsp;
as an appropriate bookend to Bracco's pivotal role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.760
in "The Sopranos."

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.680
Longtime Bruce Springsteen guitarist Steven Van&nbsp;
Zandt starred as Silvio Dante in "The Sopranos,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.640
and it was obvious from the pilot that he was&nbsp;
sporting a mega toupee. But who was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.080
have guts to call him out on it? After all,&nbsp;
he was Tony's consigliere and best buddy.&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.800
Even so, those fans longing for official&nbsp;
confirmation of Silvio's balding noggin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.000
finally get a look at it in "The Many Saints&nbsp;
of Newark." Played by John Magaro — who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.240
earlier this year played the young version&nbsp;
of Harvey Keitel-portrayed gangster Meyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.760
Lansky in the biopic "Lansky" — Silvio and&nbsp;
his combover hairstyle appear in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.840
as well as scenes where he's wearing a&nbsp;
bouffant hairpiece. To top things off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.640
there's a hilarious scene in the film that&nbsp;
shows the early failings of Silvio's toupee.&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.280
Presumably the techniques that held Silvio's&nbsp;
hairdo together in "The Sopranos" advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.400
quite a bit since the early '70s to prevent any&nbsp;
embarrassing moments. That still doesn't take away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.200
from the combover and toupee scenes in "The Many&nbsp;
Saints of Newark," which provide amusing homages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.840
to Silvio's larger-than-life mane in the series.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.760
While Vera Farmiga's version of the&nbsp;
young Livia Soprano finds her in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.440
early phases of the character who&nbsp;
would become the monstrous matriarch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.520
played by the late Nancy Marchand in&nbsp;
"The Sopranos," David Chase apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.440
couldn't resist throwing in the character's&nbsp;
signature line in "The Many Saints of Newark."&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.280
"Oh, poor you!"
Given some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.000
strife Livia encounters in "The Many Saints of&nbsp;
Newark," it's no wonder Livia grew bitter as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.120
she got older and had zero patience for crybabies&nbsp;
— especially given how she endured her husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.160
Johnny Boy's reckless and violent behavior.
When Farmiga delivers the classic line in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.320
"The Many Saints of Newark," it's naturally&nbsp;
during an argument with the iteration of Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.000
played by Michael Gandolfini. Farmiga clearly&nbsp;
studied Marchand's mocking delivery, as her "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.120
poor you!" is so spot on that it feels like it was&nbsp;
directly lifted from the actor's dialogue in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.240
"Sopranos" sound archives.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.320
While "The Many Saints of Newark" includes&nbsp;
younger versions of "Sopranos" characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.480
fans almost got to see an original cast member&nbsp;
of "The Sopranos" in the film. In the weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.880
leading up to the release of "The Many Saints of&nbsp;
Newark," director Alan Taylor revealed that Edie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.680
Falco shot a scene as Carmela for the film,&nbsp;
but it ended up on the cutting room floor.&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.320
Still, a character who appears to be Tony's&nbsp;
future wife is featured briefly in "The Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.720
Saints of Newark," and can even be seen at&nbsp;
the beginning of the film's first trailer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.360
In the scene, Tony is calling Dickie Moltisanti&nbsp;
from a pay phone booth to ask his mentor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.440
purchase him some beer for a party. One&nbsp;
of Tony's two friends standing by is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.480
blonde high schooler who he later addresses as&nbsp;
Carmela. As any "Sopranos" fan could tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.120
Tony and Carmela go on to marry, but there's&nbsp;
no real indication in the scene that the teens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.080
are a couple yet.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.960
Without divulging who the victim is, there's a&nbsp;
funeral scene in "The Many Saints of Newark,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.000
and among the mourners is a distinctive-looking&nbsp;
individual that "Sopranos" fans will recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.560
as series creator David Chase. While&nbsp;
Chase has predominantly been a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.240
and producer throughout his career —&nbsp;
and taken a few turns as a director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.360
including "The Sopranos" pilot — he also popped&nbsp;
up in numerous brief cameos in "The Sopranos."&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.040
Basically, Chase has taken the Alfred&nbsp;
Hitchcock approach to his cameos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.520
as the famed mystery director would mostly&nbsp;
only appear in subtle walkthroughs in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.600
films. Not nearly as prolific as the&nbsp;
Master of Suspense in the cameo realm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.560
Chase only turned up in three "Sopranos"&nbsp;
episodes during the series' run — in 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.200
2004, and 2006 — in which he was&nbsp;
nondescriptly billed, respectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.240
as "Italian Man," "Man" (when he was just a&nbsp;
voice on the phone) and "Airline Passenger."&nbsp;

